2026-04-27T19:35:00Z | [sandbox] | After `pnpm deploy-all` ran cleanly and all 8 containers report healthy, the indexer's GET /get_pools still returns [] empty list — pools created at 19:06 are not picked up by the indexer that started ~12min before pool creation; suggests indexer doesn't backfill on-chain history when started early or when pools are created after its initial sync. Mitigation: `docker restart deepbook-indexer deepbook-server` to force a re-index, or wait/re-deploy.
2026-04-27T19:35:00Z | [sandbox] | The sandbox bootstrap is documented as "single command" (`pnpm deploy-all`) but the deploy script's phasing (Phase 4 starts indexer; Phase 5 creates pools) means the indexer can be running before its data sources exist. No documented "wait for indexer to be ready" gate between phases.
2026-04-27T19:35:00Z | [sandbox] | Indexer error messages on missing pools are inconsistent: `/orderbook/SUI_USDC` returns "Internal error: Record not found", `/trades/SUI_USDC` returns "Resource not found: Pool 'SUI_USDC'", `/ticker` returns "Invalid request: No valid pool names provided" (no pool name was given!). Three different failure modes for "indexer doesn't know about this pool" makes it harder to handle uniformly in client code (Slot 1).
2026-04-27T19:42:00Z | [sandbox] | `pnpm deploy-all` fails at Phase 3/6 (publish token package). Build itself succeeds with only deprecation WARNINGS (sui::coin::create_currency vs coin_registry::new_currency_with_otw), but the underlying `sui client test-publish` returns non-zero. The deploy script prints "Failed to publish token" and the build output but SWALLOWS the actual transaction error message — there's no way from the script output alone to tell whether the failure is (a) the deprecated coin API now being a hard error in the sui-tools image, (b) a chain version mismatch, (c) something else. Critical DevX friction: a fresh, clean bootstrap fails opaquely.
2026-04-27T19:42:00Z | [sandbox] | The token package's deep.move:43 uses `sui::coin::create_currency` (deprecated per W04037) and deep.move:68 uses `#[allow(lint(share_owned))]` (unknown warning filter per W10007). These are upstream-bundled `.external-packages/token/sources/deep.move` issues that should be fixed at the sandbox level, not workarounded by every consumer. The fact that the bundled `token` package can't be republished against a current sui-tools image is a meta-friction: the very dependencies the sandbox is meant to make reproducible are themselves out of date.
2026-04-27T19:42:00Z | [sandbox] | Bootstrap halt: G-Boot gate from spec.md "Decision Gates" cannot pass — the indexer has no pool data (because of an earlier-stale postgres state) AND a fresh `pnpm deploy-all` does not recover it (because publish-token fails). Forge cycle execution is blocked here pending out-of-band sandbox repair.
2026-04-27T20:55:00Z | [sandbox] | After user repaired the publish-token issue and re-ran `pnpm deploy-all` successfully (script reports "SUI_USDC pool created: 0x7b8e... DEEP_SUI pool created: 0x93b9..."), GET /get_pools STILL returns []. Diagnosis: indexer postgres state survived the redeploy. Pool creation tx (`ETJ6dx5...`) committed at checkpoint 4966; indexer's pruning window has earliest_checkpoint advancing alongside chain (was 4710 at first probe, 5298 at second probe ~10min later, chain at 5305). Pool-creation event is in the indexer's permanent blind spot — it pruned past 4966 before noticing pools were created. `FIRST_CHECKPOINT=0` in .env is ignored because postgres cursor says "already past that."
2026-04-27T20:55:00Z | [sandbox] | The deploy-all flow has a DevX bug: Phase 4 (start indexer) → Phase 5 (create pools) only produces a usable indexer if the indexer was wiped between runs. `pnpm deploy-all` does NOT `docker compose down -v` first — so on every subsequent run, the indexer's pruning window has already advanced past where pools will be created. Fresh-genesis chain + stale-cursor indexer = pools-on-chain-but-not-in-indexer. The deploy script reports success because it doesn't validate the indexer's pool list as a post-deploy gate.
2026-04-27T20:55:00Z | [sandbox] | Repair recipe (for future reference / RUNBOOK): `cd ~/workspace/deepbook-sandbox/sandbox && docker compose down -v && pnpm deploy-all`. The `-v` flag wipes postgres volumes (including the indexer's checkpoint cursor), letting it bootstrap from FIRST_CHECKPOINT=0 and observe pool creation in real time. **Update 2026-04-27T21:25Z:** This recipe DOES wipe the volumes (verified — `sandbox_postgres_data` is fresh) but the indexer's pools-table-empty problem persists for a different reason — see next entry.
2026-04-27T21:25:00Z | [sandbox] [deepbook] | DEFINITIVE ROOT CAUSE for "indexer returns no pools" — it is NOT a checkpoint cursor issue. Diagnosis via direct postgres inspection: `pool_created` event table HAS 2 rows (DEEP/SUI + SUI/USDC pool-creation events at chain checkpoint 507), `deepbook_pool_registered` HAS 1 row (the SUI/USDC margin registration). But the `pools` TABLE — which `/get_pools`, `/orderbook/<name>`, `/trades/<name>`, `/ticker?pool_names=...` ALL read from (verified in `external/deepbook/crates/server/src/reader.rs:181` and downstream queries) — has 0 rows. The `pools` table schema requires curated metadata fields (`base_asset_symbol`, `base_asset_name`, `base_asset_decimals`, etc.) that are NOT present in the on-chain `pool_created` event. NOTHING in the sandbox repo (`grep -rn "INSERT INTO pools" ./sandbox/` returns no results; `scripts/utils/pool.ts` only does on-chain `register_deepbook_pool` calls, never touches postgres) populates this table. Net effect: the entire sandbox-bootstrapped state has working on-chain pools BUT a non-functional indexer REST surface for any pool-keyed endpoint. This is a fundamental gap between what the indexer schema implies the REST surface should support and what `pnpm deploy-all` actually delivers.
2026-04-27T21:25:00Z | [sandbox] | Slot 1 architecture impact: the spec's design ("React+Vite app polling `:9008` indexer for pool stats") is non-functional out of the box against a fresh sandbox. Three viable workarounds: (a) Slot 1 reads on-chain directly via Sui RPC `sui_getObject(poolId)` and parses pool order book from the object's dynamic fields — bypasses the broken indexer entirely; (b) manually `INSERT INTO pools` to seed metadata for the 2 sandbox-created pools — unblocks the indexer surface but is a one-off hack a real user wouldn't know to perform; (c) skip Slot 1 entirely; ship only Slot 2 + Slot 3. None of (a/b/c) is actually using the sandbox's indexer surface as advertised — that's a Slot 1 deliverable scope-cut driven by sandbox bug.
2026-04-27T21:25:00Z | [sandbox] | Suggested upstream fix the sandbox team can land: extend `scripts/utils/pool.ts::createDeepbookPools` (or a new helper) to also `INSERT INTO pools` after each on-chain pool creation, populating `base_asset_symbol/decimals/name` from `coin::CoinMetadata` (which the deploy already publishes per the manifest's `0x...::coin::CoinMetadata<...>` entries) and `tick_size/lot_size/min_size` from the args passed to the on-chain pool constructor. This is a 30-50 LOC change that would make `pnpm deploy-all` produce a fully-functional indexer surface end-to-end.
2026-04-27T21:47:00Z | [sui-sdk] | `suix_queryEvents` `MoveModule` filter selects on `transactionModule` (the module that emits, e.g. `pool`), not the event struct's declaring module — filtering on `module: "order_info"` returns zero events even though the event type symbol is `0x...::order_info::OrderInfo`. Slot 1 must filter on `module: "pool"` for any DeepBook event read. Not documented in the @mysten/sui SDK 2.x event-fetching docs we consulted.
2026-04-27T21:47:00Z | [deepbook] | DeepBook v3 `book::Book` stores asks/bids as `BigVector<Order>` (custom container; not a stdlib type). The slice objects (`big_vector::Slice<Order>`) live as dynamic fields under the BigVector's UID and churn rapidly during market-maker activity (sub-second deletion + recreation). A naive read sequence `suix_getDynamicFields → sui_getObject(slice)` races and frequently returns `{ error: { code: "deleted" } }`. Production reads need either retry logic or a single-tx read path; this is undocumented in the sandbox README.
2026-04-27T21:47:00Z | [deepbook] | The bundled sandbox market-maker (`scripts/market-maker/`) places + cancels grid orders but never matches its own orders, so `OrderInfo.fills[]` is always empty against an idle sandbox. Verifying live-fill UI behaviour (Slot 1 sparkline, 24h volume) requires running a manual market-mover trade — not obvious from the sandbox README, and there's no out-of-the-box `pnpm fill-once` helper.
2026-04-27T22:55:00Z | [deepbook] | DeepBook v3 order_id encoding: high bit (bit 127) is the ask flag (set for asks, clear for bids); high 64 bits below the flag are the price; low 64 bits are an order-counter disambiguator. Captured chain examples: bid 12377783720203182843884075 → price 671000 (no inversion); ask 170141183460486719245069180370816077150 = 2^127 + (948000 << 64) + counter → masking bit 127 then >>64 yields 948000. The chain-shape.md prose ("inverted for bids") in the bundled DeepBook docs was misleading and contradicted by the captured data; the correct decoder is `(BigInt(orderId) & ((1n << 127n) - 1n)) >> 64n` for both sides. This took 4 cycle iterations to get right against the multi-reviewer pass; the pattern of "structural fix that doesn't actually wire through" repeated twice (iter-2 added the field, iter-3 used wrong formula).
2026-04-27T22:55:00Z | [sui-sdk] | TypeScript LSP from a worktree-root cwd does not index sub-package node_modules cleanly. Across 4 iterations, recurring stale "Cannot find module 'react'/'vitest'" diagnostics appeared in spec-defined IDE feedback even though `pnpm test` resolved cleanly inside the sub-package. Required ignoring the LSP signal in favor of the test-runner truth.
2026-04-27T22:55:00Z | [forge-process] | The forge protocol's strict `cycle-pass.sh` gate ("zero critical AND zero disputed clusters") is hard to satisfy without coordinating reviewer severity scales — which would defeat the independence the multi-reviewer design relies on. iter-4 of Cycle 1 had 0 critical + 0 triangulated correctness regressions but still failed the gate on 4 disputed clusters that were meta-disagreements about severity (one was even disputed about whether the iter-4 fix was a positive observation). User accepted the cycle DELIVERED-WITH-CAVEATS at the retry cap. NOT a DeepBook DevX issue — filed as forge-process for the v0.2.0 maintainers.
2026-04-27T23:35:00Z | [move] | The new (sui 1.69.x) Move package manager refuses to compile any package that depends on a package declaring `[environments]` (e.g. the bundled deepbook/token packages declare `localnet = "..."`), unless the consuming package also declares an `[environments]` block AND the build is run with `--build-env <name>`. With NO `[environments]` block, build/test fail with "Could not determine the correct dependencies to use for `localnet`; pass one of `--build-env testnet` or `--build-env mainnet`" — but neither testnet nor mainnet is what we want, and there's no `--build-env localnet` option visible until we add the block ourselves. Resolution that worked for cycle-2 red: add `[environments]\nlocalnet = "a62c4e17"` to Move.toml and invoke `sui move test --build-env localnet`. The chain-id value need not match the running sandbox's chain identifier for `sui move build`/`test` (which doesn't talk to a node), only for `sui client publish`.
2026-04-27T23:35:00Z | [deepbook] | The bundled `.external-packages/deepbook_margin/tests/helper/test_helpers.move:435` calls `pyth::price_info::new_price_info_object_for_test` — a function that does NOT exist in the bundled `packages/pyth/sources/price_info.move`. This breaks `sui move test` for ANY package that depends on `deepbook_margin` (including the canonical `example_contract` template). example_contract itself can't `sui move test` clean against the bundled sandbox state. Cycle-2 green-phase implementer will inherit this and need a workaround (e.g. add the missing test-only function to the local pyth package, or restrict the test build to skip deepbook_margin's test sources, or stub the test_helper). Not caused by Slot 2.
2026-04-27T23:55:00Z | [deepbook] | Cycle-2 green phase confirmed: `deepbook::pool::swap_exact_base_for_quote` (no-manager path, lines 248-266 of pool.move) takes `(self, base_in, deep_in, min_quote_out, clock, ctx)` and returns `(Coin<BaseAsset>, Coin<QuoteAsset>, Coin<DEEP>)`. Passing `min_quote_out = 0` suppresses DeepBook's own internal slippage check, allowing the wrapper to own the abort gate via its own `assert!`. Critical for T-002 which pins the abort code to `slippage_swap::EInsufficientOutput` rather than `deepbook::pool::EMinimumQuantityOutNotMet` (code 12).
2026-04-27T23:55:00Z | [move] | Move test_scenario's shared-object store does NOT fully reset between two separate `begin(addr)` / `end(scenario)` pairs executed sequentially within the same `#[test]` function. The Clock singleton (@0x6) and possibly other shared-object UIDs created in the first scenario persist in the global test VM store; calling `setup_everything` a second time (as T-003 does) tries to create Registry and other objects whose `versioned::create` dynamic-field-add fails with `EFieldAlreadyExists` because the first scenario's objects (same UID, due to counter reset at `begin`) are still in the store. Result: T-003 (`swap_succeeds_when_min_out_equals_realised`) is structurally unpassable in a two-scenario pattern within one test function — not an implementation bug.
2026-04-28T00:30:00Z | [move] | Move test_scenario CLEARS the event buffer on every `next_tx` — `event::events_by_type<>()` only sees events emitted in the current tx. Tests that emit an event in tx N and want to inspect it must do so BEFORE calling `next_tx`. Locals persist across `next_tx` blocks, so cross-tx checks (e.g., `vault_id == event_vault_id` where the vault becomes takeable in the next tx) work by stashing event values into locals and re-checking after the take. Caught Cycle 3 iter-1 by 4/7 tests failing on `events.length() == 1` after the tx that emitted them. Not documented in `.move-book-docs/book/testing/test-scenario.md` — only callable from `test_scenario_tests.move:1092` (`assert_eq!(event::num_events(), 0)` after `next_tx`).
2026-04-28T00:30:00Z | [move] | Permissionless-but-conditional Move entry functions need explicit object-binding asserts. Cycle 3 vault stored `pool_id: ID` but `execute_trigger` never asserted `object::id(pool) == vault.pool_id` — anyone could substitute any same-typed pool and (with min_quote_out=0 by design) drain the vault for ~zero quote. Triangulated as high by 3 of 6 reviewers in iter-1; fix is one line. Generalizable lesson: any time a Move struct stores an ID that's meant to bind a function arg, write the assert at the entry.
